 1 big thing: Your midterm cheat sheet 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Axios' Hans Nichols has your insider's guide to Tuesday night's midterm results: 

 You won't need to stay up all night to spot a big Democratic wave. But it could take days, or even weeks, to know whether Republicans held the Senate.

 🏛️ What we're watching for the House: Florida, Florida, Florida.

 Most polls close at 7 p.m. ET, and the state counts quickly. 🐊 At 8:01 p.m. ET, refresh your browser and watch FL-14. The great mid-cycle redistricting wars transformed this Tampa-based district from Harris +8 to Trump +11.

 If Democratic Rep. Kathy Castor survives, Republicans can all but kiss their majority goodbye. Next up is FL-25. The country's most expensive general election race is playing out in a district that went from Harris +4 to Trump +9. If Democratic Rep. Jared Moskowitz's race is close — or too close to call early in the night — House Republicans can keep hope alive. This is also a good time to check on South Carolina's Senate race, where polls close at 7 p.m. ET and the count comes relatively fast.

 If Sen. Darline Graham loses to Annie Andrews in the Trump +18 state, Senate Republicans can wave goodbye to their majority. (And they'll be sweating if it's close.) "Underperformance is rarely isolated," said Jason Thielman, executive director of the National Republican Senatorial Committee in 2024. "If you see the GOP underperforming early in the East, it is likely going to continue as you move farther West." 🐎 At 9 p.m. ET, check in on Virginia: The commonwealth's polls close at 7 p.m. ET, but tight races often aren't called until the 9 p.m. hour.

 If Democrats flip VA-01, a Trump +5 seat, they're in for a very good night. If they flip VA-05, a Trump +12 seat, they're in for a great night. Between the lines: VA-02 — dominated by Virginia Beach, and one of the nation's most competitive House seats — will tell us whether a strong Republican candidate can survive a Democratic wave.

 GOP strategists believe Republican Rep. Jen Kiggans (in a rematch against Democrat Elaine Luria, who lost the seat in 2022) has run a nearly flawless campaign. If even Kiggans gets swept away in a 50-50 district, other Republicans in a low-single-digit Trump seat should brace for impact. Both women are Navy veterans . If Kiggans holds on, that could bode well for Sen. Susan Collins in Maine and Rep. Ashley Hinson in the Iowa Senate race. 👢 Moving west: Polls close in Ohio at 7:30 p.m. ET and in Texas at 8 p.m. ET, but Texas counts faster.

 If Texas Attorney General Ken Paxton  holds on, Republicans still have a path to keeping their Senate majority. Iowa polls close at 9 p.m. ET. If Hinson is looking good, the night might not be as bad as many Republicans feared. Pro tip: Don't stay up for the Senate results in either Maine or Alaska.

 Even a clear win (or defeat) for Collins may not be obvious until Wednesday. In 2020, when she won by nearly 9 points, the AP didn't call the race until lunchtime Wednesday. Alaska, with its ranked-choice voting , could take weeks. Share this guide . 

 
 
